00:04:30.470 --> 00:04:32.815
As we talk about high
availability and disaster

00:04:32.815 --> 00:04:34.940
recovery, it's important
to start with a little bit

00:04:34.940 --> 00:04:36.440
of terminology.

00:04:36.440 --> 00:04:39.320
To begin, we'll
talk about backups.

00:04:39.320 --> 00:04:42.560
Backups are independent
copies of the data set

00:04:42.560 --> 00:04:44.840
that isn't in the
database serving path.

00:04:44.840 --> 00:04:48.020
This means we think about the
database's data set, the data

00:04:48.020 --> 00:04:49.650
it's working on right now.

00:04:49.650 --> 00:04:51.950
We usually make a copy of
it-- an independent copy--

00:04:51.950 --> 00:04:53.780
sent to some other
storage system

00:04:53.780 --> 00:04:56.960
so that that backup is no
longer in the serving path

00:04:56.960 --> 00:04:59.030
or the place where the
database is making changes

00:04:59.030 --> 00:05:00.770
to the primary data set.

00:05:00.770 --> 00:05:03.540
In Cloud SQL, we offer
automated backups,

00:05:03.540 --> 00:05:05.840
which means we take
them daily for example,

00:05:05.840 --> 00:05:07.490
at a time that you set.

00:05:07.490 --> 00:05:10.130
We also offer on-demand or
manual backups, where you

00:05:10.130 --> 00:05:12.050
can tell us to take a backup.

00:05:12.050 --> 00:05:13.990
And we'll take it in that one.

00:05:13.990 --> 00:05:17.050
Going beyond backups, we get
to point-in-time recovery.

00:05:17.050 --> 00:05:19.390
Point-in-time recovery
is functionality that

00:05:19.390 --> 00:05:24.940
often uses backups to restore to
a very granular point in time,

00:05:24.940 --> 00:05:27.250
down to, for example,
the millisecond.

00:05:27.250 --> 00:05:28.780
As part of
point-in-time recovery,

00:05:28.780 --> 00:05:34.060
usually we keep transaction logs
which contain a list or a log

00:05:34.060 --> 00:05:36.430
of all transactions the
database has been running--

00:05:36.430 --> 00:05:38.470
all the inserts,
updates, et cetera--

00:05:38.470 --> 00:05:41.830
so that we can go back to
very granular points in time,

00:05:41.830 --> 00:05:43.600
specific moments when
a transaction was

00:05:43.600 --> 00:05:45.550
run, for example.

00:05:45.550 --> 00:05:47.560
Also on the list is replication.

00:05:47.560 --> 00:05:51.870
Replication is a continuous
copy of data from your source

00:05:51.870 --> 00:05:54.970
or your primary
database to a replica

00:05:54.970 --> 00:05:56.900
or a secondary database.

00:05:56.900 --> 00:06:00.010
This means that we
are making sure we

00:06:00.010 --> 00:06:02.110
have either a real time
or a near real time

00:06:02.110 --> 00:06:06.020
copy of data in some other
database from your primary.

00:06:06.020 --> 00:06:07.680
Replication has
two major options.

00:06:07.680 --> 00:06:08.990
One is synchronous.

00:06:08.990 --> 00:06:10.400
The other is asynchronous.

00:06:10.400 --> 00:06:12.620
In a synchronous
replication model,

00:06:12.620 --> 00:06:15.830
a transaction is
not fully committed

00:06:15.830 --> 00:06:18.890
or is not responded to by the
primary instance to a client

00:06:18.890 --> 00:06:23.540
until both the primary database
instance and its replica

00:06:23.540 --> 00:06:25.070
have committed the transaction.

00:06:25.070 --> 00:06:27.260
The primary instance
will wait for the replica

00:06:27.260 --> 00:06:30.500
to ensure that both have
committed the transaction

00:06:30.500 --> 00:06:33.620
before it responds to the
client saying your transaction

00:06:33.620 --> 00:06:35.270
has now been committed.

00:06:35.270 --> 00:06:38.880
In asynchronous replication,
the primary instance

00:06:38.880 --> 00:06:40.460
doesn't wait for the replica.

00:06:40.460 --> 00:06:42.450
In this case, the
primary instance

00:06:42.450 --> 00:06:44.590
simply commits the transaction.

00:06:44.590 --> 00:06:46.310
And without waiting
for the replica

00:06:46.310 --> 00:06:49.190
to let it know that it too
committed the transaction,

00:06:49.190 --> 00:06:52.550
it responds to the client
saying your transaction has

00:06:52.550 --> 00:06:53.660
been committed.

00:06:53.660 --> 00:06:55.310
We'll talk about
how Cloud SQL uses

00:06:55.310 --> 00:06:59.060
both of these as part of its
availability and disaster

00:06:59.060 --> 00:07:02.390
recovery protection mechanisms.

00:07:02.390 --> 00:07:04.550
The last two terms
that we'll go over here

00:07:04.550 --> 00:07:08.090
are recovery point objective
and recovery time objective.

00:07:08.090 --> 00:07:12.170
Recovery point objective
is the maximum time period

00:07:12.170 --> 00:07:16.850
in which we might lose data from
our system due to an incident.

00:07:16.850 --> 00:07:19.100
Spoken plainly,
this means that when

00:07:19.100 --> 00:07:23.390
we take a backup right now, we
allow the database to continue

00:07:23.390 --> 00:07:23.940
to run.

00:07:23.940 --> 00:07:25.880
So our backup in
this very moment

00:07:25.880 --> 00:07:28.130
has the exact data
set our database does.

00:07:28.130 --> 00:07:31.130
We have a full copy,
an independent copy,

00:07:31.130 --> 00:07:33.390
of the data set from right now.

00:07:33.390 --> 00:07:36.890
But the database continues to
perform inserts and updates,

00:07:36.890 --> 00:07:40.070
changing the data set
subsequent to our backup.

00:07:40.070 --> 00:07:41.960
Now there's data in
the database that we

00:07:41.960 --> 00:07:43.730
don't have in our backup.

00:07:43.730 --> 00:07:46.880
If we were to have an incident
losing our database right now,

00:07:46.880 --> 00:07:49.670
we would lose all of
the transactions that

00:07:49.670 --> 00:07:53.000
were committed after
we took our backup.

00:07:53.000 --> 00:07:55.040
When we talk about a
recovery point objective,

00:07:55.040 --> 00:07:56.940
we're setting an
objective which states

00:07:56.940 --> 00:08:00.570
we are only wanting to lose
a particular amount of data.

00:08:00.570 --> 00:08:03.920
So for example, if our
RPO were five minutes,

00:08:03.920 --> 00:08:05.930
we would ensure that we
have a data protection

00:08:05.930 --> 00:08:08.240
mechanism in place
such that we would not

00:08:08.240 --> 00:08:11.550
lose more than five minutes
of transactions for example,

00:08:11.550 --> 00:08:13.850
from one backup to the next.

00:08:13.850 --> 00:08:15.920
This is depicted in the
diagram on the slide

00:08:15.920 --> 00:08:17.990
as a backup, which
is 0.1, and then

00:08:17.990 --> 00:08:20.870
an incident happening roughly
in the middle of the diagram.

00:08:20.870 --> 00:08:23.570
The difference or the
timeline between the backup

00:08:23.570 --> 00:08:25.450
and the incident is--

00:08:25.450 --> 00:08:28.100
it helps us define our recovery
point objective effectively

00:08:28.100 --> 00:08:31.550
quantifies how much data
we've lost in that incident.

00:08:31.550 --> 00:08:36.320
Recovery time objective is
the targeted duration of time

00:08:36.320 --> 00:08:40.370
that we allow to recover
the data that has previously

00:08:40.370 --> 00:08:41.360
been backed up.

00:08:41.360 --> 00:08:44.090
What this means is in
the event of an incident,

00:08:44.090 --> 00:08:46.520
when we want to use one of
our data protection mechanisms

00:08:46.520 --> 00:08:50.550
to recover data, we
measure the amount of time

00:08:50.550 --> 00:08:54.910
it takes to perform the recovery
from the backup as an example,

00:08:54.910 --> 00:08:56.940
and get the database
back up and running.

00:08:56.940 --> 00:08:59.610
That amount of time is
known as our recovery time.

00:08:59.610 --> 00:09:01.350
And we set an
objective which states

00:09:01.350 --> 00:09:04.860
how much time we will allow
for that process to happen.

00:09:04.860 --> 00:09:08.340
In a critical database
system, usually the RPO,

00:09:08.340 --> 00:09:12.000
recovery point objective, and
RTO are pretty small values.

00:09:12.000 --> 00:09:14.490
Recovery point objective
perhaps, measured in seconds.

00:09:14.490 --> 00:09:17.400
And recovery time objective
perhaps, measured in minutes.

00:09:17.400 --> 00:09:20.280
In less critical systems
or non-critical databases,

00:09:20.280 --> 00:09:22.470
these objectives may
be much more lenient,

00:09:22.470 --> 00:09:24.690
with recovery point
objectives being much larger

00:09:24.690 --> 00:09:26.720
and recovery time
objectives being longer,

00:09:26.720 --> 00:09:30.840
or allowing more data loss
and more recovery time.

00:09:30.840 --> 00:09:34.350
And we define how we protect
data-- the mechanisms we use,

00:09:34.350 --> 00:09:36.330
the number of protections
we put in place--

00:09:36.330 --> 00:09:41.470
to try to adhere to
our RPO and our RTO.

00:09:41.470 --> 00:09:44.920
Let's talk about protecting
against user error.

00:09:44.920 --> 00:09:48.160
In presentations
like these, often we

00:09:48.160 --> 00:09:49.640
talk mostly about disasters.

00:09:49.640 --> 00:09:51.940
And we depict them with
lightning bolts or fire

00:09:51.940 --> 00:09:53.110
on the slides.

00:09:53.110 --> 00:09:56.290
In reality, much more
common that human error

00:09:56.290 --> 00:09:57.700
is the culprit of data loss.

00:09:57.700 --> 00:10:00.160
We ourselves, in some
sense, are the disaster.

00:10:00.160 --> 00:10:03.610
Perhaps I make a mistake, I
drop a table, change a row,

00:10:03.610 --> 00:10:05.030
and I didn't mean to.

00:10:05.030 --> 00:10:06.500
I want to get back in time.

00:10:06.500 --> 00:10:08.147
I want to undo that change.

00:10:08.147 --> 00:10:09.730
And I may not know,
by the way, that I

00:10:09.730 --> 00:10:12.940
made a mistake for a
couple hours, for example.

00:10:12.940 --> 00:10:15.775
How do we protect against
that kind of user error, which

00:10:15.775 --> 00:10:19.090
is far more common in
enterprise IT environments

00:10:19.090 --> 00:10:21.580
or any IT environment?

00:10:21.580 --> 00:10:24.510
Cloud SQL, I talked
about using backups.

00:10:24.510 --> 00:10:27.560
In Cloud SQL, as mentioned,
offers both automated backups,

00:10:27.560 --> 00:10:29.065
as well as on demand backups.

00:10:29.065 --> 00:10:30.440
In the screenshot,
we show a user

00:10:30.440 --> 00:10:33.230
configuring automated backups
where the user is setting

00:10:33.230 --> 00:10:35.450
a time, when we take
a backup automatically

00:10:35.450 --> 00:10:37.820
via Cloud SQL once per day.

00:10:37.820 --> 00:10:39.980
With these backups,
it's important to note

00:10:39.980 --> 00:10:42.800
that these are storage-level
or block-level backups,

00:10:42.800 --> 00:10:46.160
so that we are minimizing
the amount of data

00:10:46.160 --> 00:10:47.870
that we are tracking
between backups,

00:10:47.870 --> 00:10:50.090
or that we are saving
to another system.

00:10:50.090 --> 00:10:52.070
If we take a backup
at this moment,

00:10:52.070 --> 00:10:54.890
we watch and track the
number of block changes

00:10:54.890 --> 00:10:56.930
to the next time we
take a backup, which

00:10:56.930 --> 00:10:58.460
means the size of backups--

00:10:58.460 --> 00:11:00.050
and common question
from customers

00:11:00.050 --> 00:11:01.910
is, how large will
my backups be--

00:11:01.910 --> 00:11:04.970
it is more dependent
on how busy or active

00:11:04.970 --> 00:11:07.760
your database is, than
on how frequently you

00:11:07.760 --> 00:11:09.140
take these backups.

00:11:09.140 --> 00:11:11.660
For example, a very
active database

00:11:11.660 --> 00:11:15.350
will generate quite large backup
files, lots of block changes,

00:11:15.350 --> 00:11:18.620
from one backup to the next,
whereas a largely inactive

00:11:18.620 --> 00:11:22.100
database will generate many
changes between backups.

00:11:22.100 --> 00:11:24.260
And even if we take
backups quite frequently,

00:11:24.260 --> 00:11:25.370
they might be very small.

00:11:25.370 --> 00:11:28.520
You can imagine a database
not doing any work right now.

00:11:28.520 --> 00:11:30.440
If we take a backup
and wait a few seconds

00:11:30.440 --> 00:11:32.570
and then take another
one, the second backup

00:11:32.570 --> 00:11:34.610
may be extremely
small, because we

00:11:34.610 --> 00:11:37.788
didn't have any subsequent block
changes between the backups.

00:11:37.788 --> 00:11:39.580
How can we do a little
better than backups?

00:11:39.580 --> 00:11:41.560
If we're taking
backups each day,

00:11:41.560 --> 00:11:43.630
that might mean we
have a recovery point

00:11:43.630 --> 00:11:47.290
objective of roughly 24
hours, where our worst case is

00:11:47.290 --> 00:11:50.320
that if we take a backup
yesterday at 11:00 PM,

00:11:50.320 --> 00:11:53.170
we have a problem
today at 10:59 PM,

00:11:53.170 --> 00:11:56.050
almost exactly 24 hours
since our last backup,

00:11:56.050 --> 00:11:58.960
but about a minute before
we take the next backup.

00:11:58.960 --> 00:12:00.470
How can we do better than this?

00:12:00.470 --> 00:12:02.920
This is where point-in-time
recovery comes into play.

00:12:02.920 --> 00:12:05.620
As I mentioned, point-in-time
recovery builds upon backups,

00:12:05.620 --> 00:12:08.830
or uses backups usually as
part of the recovery mechanism.

00:12:08.830 --> 00:12:11.710
Here, we're showing a screenshot
of setting up or rather,

00:12:11.710 --> 00:12:14.470
recovering using point-in-time
recovery in Cloud SQL.

00:12:14.470 --> 00:12:17.860
What you see here is that
we're using a timestamp based

00:12:17.860 --> 00:12:18.820
point-in-time.

00:12:18.820 --> 00:12:21.130
Here, we're selecting
down to the millisecond

00:12:21.130 --> 00:12:23.380
when we want to recover
to it in the past.

00:12:23.380 --> 00:12:27.670
Cloud SQL offers transaction
or timestamp-based recovery

00:12:27.670 --> 00:12:29.890
with point-in-time
recovery back seven days.

00:12:29.890 --> 00:12:31.720
So it allows me to
pick a millisecond

00:12:31.720 --> 00:12:35.140
in any of the last seven days
to which I want to recover to.

00:12:35.140 --> 00:12:37.390
This means that if I've made
a mistake, as I described

00:12:37.390 --> 00:12:40.000
earlier, I might want to go
to the millisecond or even

00:12:40.000 --> 00:12:42.240
the second before I
made that mistake.

00:12:42.240 --> 00:12:44.440
And in this example,
we're cloning or copying

00:12:44.440 --> 00:12:45.670
a database to that point.

00:12:45.670 --> 00:12:47.920
So I can go back and get
that data, bring it back

00:12:47.920 --> 00:12:50.590
into my primary database,
effectively undoing

00:12:50.590 --> 00:12:52.870
the mistake that I made.

00:12:52.870 --> 00:12:55.120
With backup protections
or data protections

00:12:55.120 --> 00:12:57.250
rather, in mind,
let's now take a look

00:12:57.250 --> 00:12:59.650
at how to achieve
high availability.

00:12:59.650 --> 00:13:01.330
As a starting
point, you all have

00:13:01.330 --> 00:13:03.730
worked in IT environments
for a long time.

00:13:03.730 --> 00:13:06.070
And you know that high
availability is not

00:13:06.070 --> 00:13:08.080
easy to achieve it
usually involves

00:13:08.080 --> 00:13:11.530
some combination of replication,
health checking, and then

00:13:11.530 --> 00:13:13.030
some workflow
management, in case

00:13:13.030 --> 00:13:14.680
we want to automatically
fail failover

00:13:14.680 --> 00:13:16.660
in the event of an incident.

00:13:16.660 --> 00:13:18.760
With high availability,
we usually

00:13:18.760 --> 00:13:22.050
begin by talking
about data redundancy.

00:13:22.050 --> 00:13:24.850
High availability often
relies on writing data

00:13:24.850 --> 00:13:27.850
to two or more places
so that in case

00:13:27.850 --> 00:13:30.860
of an incident with our
primary database instance,

00:13:30.860 --> 00:13:33.130
we have the data
elsewhere, and are quickly

00:13:33.130 --> 00:13:37.240
able to make it available to
recover from that incident.

00:13:37.240 --> 00:13:40.720
This type of configuration is
sometimes called a cluster.

00:13:40.720 --> 00:13:45.010
It simply means we're using
two or more database servers

00:13:45.010 --> 00:13:46.960
to perform the
tasks that one could

00:13:46.960 --> 00:13:50.440
perform so that
we have redundancy

00:13:50.440 --> 00:13:51.810
in case of an incident.

00:13:51.810 --> 00:13:55.120
For Cloud SQL, it's important to
note that the high availability

00:13:55.120 --> 00:13:57.820
configuration is required
for SLA coverage.

00:13:57.820 --> 00:14:00.700
Our service level
agreement for SLA

00:14:00.700 --> 00:14:02.920
guarantees a particular uptime.

00:14:02.920 --> 00:14:06.100
And as part of that SLA, we
require that high availability

00:14:06.100 --> 00:14:08.590
is enabled so that we can
automatically and quickly

00:14:08.590 --> 00:14:11.020
recover from any incident
that may affect your database

00:14:11.020 --> 00:14:13.000
instance.

00:14:13.000 --> 00:14:16.440
When we talk about high
availability in Cloud SQL,

00:14:16.440 --> 00:14:18.690
this is conceptually
what it looks like.

00:14:18.690 --> 00:14:21.960
When the high availability
configuration is enabled,

00:14:21.960 --> 00:14:24.720
we create a primary
instance in the zone

00:14:24.720 --> 00:14:26.820
and a standby instance
in another zone.

00:14:26.820 --> 00:14:29.895
In this example, we've created
a primary instance in zone A,

00:14:29.895 --> 00:14:32.970
and a standby instance
in zone B. This

00:14:32.970 --> 00:14:36.990
ensures that we have redundancy
in our HA configuration

00:14:36.990 --> 00:14:39.150
such that if there's
a problem in zone A,

00:14:39.150 --> 00:14:41.610
we have a place to failover
to, and an instance that we

00:14:41.610 --> 00:14:43.980
can bring up quickly
to recover and be back

00:14:43.980 --> 00:14:45.900
online for our client.

00:14:45.900 --> 00:14:50.070
Also shown in the diagram
is that we're writing data

00:14:50.070 --> 00:14:51.360
to two places at once.

00:14:51.360 --> 00:14:53.770
We're using a regional
persistent disk,

00:14:53.770 --> 00:14:56.010
which synchronicity
replicates data

00:14:56.010 --> 00:14:59.130
in both zones-- in this
case, zone A and zone B.

00:14:59.130 --> 00:15:01.740
So before the primary
database instance

00:15:01.740 --> 00:15:05.400
will confirm to a client that a
transaction has been committed,

00:15:05.400 --> 00:15:08.880
it ensures that the data has
been written to both zones

00:15:08.880 --> 00:15:11.250
when our system is
operating normally.

00:15:11.250 --> 00:15:15.540
This means that we can reliably
failover to our standby zone

00:15:15.540 --> 00:15:17.970
knowing we have a full
copy of the data set,

00:15:17.970 --> 00:15:19.920
and every last
committed transaction

00:15:19.920 --> 00:15:23.250
will be present when we bring
the database back online.

00:15:23.250 --> 00:15:25.140
In case of an outage,
Cloud SQL does

00:15:25.140 --> 00:15:27.810
a number of things from
a workflow perspective.

00:15:27.810 --> 00:15:29.730
Most important to
note is that we

00:15:29.730 --> 00:15:33.510
move the IP address and the
name of the primary instance

00:15:33.510 --> 00:15:35.080
to the standby instance.

00:15:35.080 --> 00:15:38.100
This is important because
an application then need not

00:15:38.100 --> 00:15:40.020
know about the failover itself.

00:15:40.020 --> 00:15:43.440
It only has to try to reconnect,
because the IP address

00:15:43.440 --> 00:15:44.490
doesn't change.

00:15:44.490 --> 00:15:46.140
To the application,
it simply looks

00:15:46.140 --> 00:15:49.540
like the database has gone
down and then has come back up.

00:15:49.540 --> 00:15:52.020
And by retrying,
the application can

00:15:52.020 --> 00:15:54.248
reconnect and begin
serving traffic just

00:15:54.248 --> 00:15:55.040
after the failover.

00:15:59.040 --> 00:16:01.060
To get used or to
get comfortable

00:16:01.060 --> 00:16:03.790
with Cloud SQL's high
availability configuration,

00:16:03.790 --> 00:16:05.470
I recommend that
you get hands-on.

00:16:05.470 --> 00:16:07.820
You can check it out for
yourself in the product.

00:16:07.820 --> 00:16:09.370
And you can initiate a failover.

00:16:09.370 --> 00:16:12.730
In the UI, you'll see a
failover button or VR API.

00:16:12.730 --> 00:16:14.590
You can also take a
look at our code lapse.

00:16:14.590 --> 00:16:16.750
We have a code lab
in particular that

00:16:16.750 --> 00:16:18.575
features Kubernetes
Engine in Cloud SQL,

00:16:18.575 --> 00:16:21.160
and it talks about how you
can build a fault tolerance

00:16:21.160 --> 00:16:24.730
application, where
in the lab, you

00:16:24.730 --> 00:16:27.410
will test fault tolerance both
in the application layer--

00:16:27.410 --> 00:16:30.790
Kubernetes engine-- as
well as in Cloud SQL.

00:16:30.790 --> 00:16:33.820
Lastly, I encourage you
to check out this showcase

00:16:33.820 --> 00:16:36.910
that we've created for
Google Cloud Next 2020.

00:16:36.910 --> 00:16:40.240
This showcase is essentially
a whack-a-mole of databases

00:16:40.240 --> 00:16:43.360
where you can see how many times
you can make Cloud SQL fail

00:16:43.360 --> 00:16:47.410
and how many times it recovers
by kind of whack-a-mole style

00:16:47.410 --> 00:16:49.390
taking down the database,
perhaps in zone A

00:16:49.390 --> 00:16:52.570
and seeing it pop back up
in zone B. The link is shown

00:16:52.570 --> 00:16:53.230
in the slide.

00:16:53.230 --> 00:16:54.688
And I encourage
you to take a look.

00:16:57.690 --> 00:16:59.970
Let's round out with
a look at recovering

00:16:59.970 --> 00:17:02.260
in case of a large disaster.

00:17:02.260 --> 00:17:05.130
In here, you and your
careers have likely

00:17:05.130 --> 00:17:07.859
spent a lot of time
planning and even testing

00:17:07.859 --> 00:17:09.140
for large disasters.

00:17:09.140 --> 00:17:11.339
Hopefully, you haven't
faced many real ones.

00:17:11.339 --> 00:17:13.619
In Google terms, when
we think of a disaster,

00:17:13.619 --> 00:17:16.930
we tend to think of this
as a regional outage, where

00:17:16.930 --> 00:17:20.040
a region is comprised of
usually three or more zones,

00:17:20.040 --> 00:17:21.207
independent failure domains.

00:17:21.207 --> 00:17:23.165
And this would mean
something has happened such

00:17:23.165 --> 00:17:25.380
that all the zones or enough
of the zones in a region

00:17:25.380 --> 00:17:28.030
are down, that the region
does not have connectivity.

00:17:28.030 --> 00:17:29.490
It is totally down.

00:17:29.490 --> 00:17:32.700
As you can imagine, this
has significant consequences

00:17:32.700 --> 00:17:33.720
as any disaster would.

00:17:33.720 --> 00:17:35.880
When you're doing your
disaster recovery planning,

00:17:35.880 --> 00:17:37.950
you're imagining
usually that most

00:17:37.950 --> 00:17:39.810
of the applications,
if not all that

00:17:39.810 --> 00:17:42.830
serve your internal users
and your external customers,

00:17:42.830 --> 00:17:43.920
may be down.

00:17:43.920 --> 00:17:46.680
And in that moment,
the job is to make sure

00:17:46.680 --> 00:17:50.220
that we can recover quickly
and perform the recovery such

00:17:50.220 --> 00:17:51.690
that we lose a
minimum set of data.

00:17:51.690 --> 00:17:53.790
A stressful moment, to be sure.

00:17:53.790 --> 00:17:55.500
Let's look at how
Cloud SQL has tried

00:17:55.500 --> 00:17:57.910
to make this a little easier.

00:17:57.910 --> 00:18:00.030
We've introduced cross
region replication

00:18:00.030 --> 00:18:02.290
to help guard against
regional failure.

00:18:02.290 --> 00:18:04.830
It allows you to
have a near real time

00:18:04.830 --> 00:18:08.790
copy via a replica in one
or more regions outside

00:18:08.790 --> 00:18:10.350
of your primary region.

00:18:10.350 --> 00:18:12.210
We're relying on
Google's network,

00:18:12.210 --> 00:18:15.960
including Google Cloud
VPC, or virtual networks,

00:18:15.960 --> 00:18:18.600
so that you have
a very private way

00:18:18.600 --> 00:18:21.480
to connect between
your primary instance

00:18:21.480 --> 00:18:23.460
and it's replicas
in other regions.

00:18:23.460 --> 00:18:26.430
Further, because
Google's VPCs are global,

00:18:26.430 --> 00:18:28.860
there's a very minimal
network configuration for you

00:18:28.860 --> 00:18:29.850
to perform here.

00:18:29.850 --> 00:18:33.510
The Google VPC spans all
regions in the cloud network,

00:18:33.510 --> 00:18:35.850
all of the regions you're
using, which makes this very

00:18:35.850 --> 00:18:37.710
easy to use and to configure.

00:18:37.710 --> 00:18:39.540
In fact, you see in
the screenshot here

00:18:39.540 --> 00:18:42.030
highlighted all we're
doing is selecting

00:18:42.030 --> 00:18:43.800
a region, other than
our primary region,

00:18:43.800 --> 00:18:45.460
when we're creating a replica.

00:18:45.460 --> 00:18:48.540
And we can be certain that
the replica is being spun up

00:18:48.540 --> 00:18:51.510
in another region, and we're
connecting from our primary

00:18:51.510 --> 00:18:53.580
to our replicate in
a very secure fashion

00:18:53.580 --> 00:18:56.250
over our private network.

00:18:56.250 --> 00:18:58.870
With the replica now
deployed in another region,

00:18:58.870 --> 00:19:02.040
let's look at what's actually
happening behind the scenes.

00:19:02.040 --> 00:19:06.570
In this diagram, you see a
high availability configuration

00:19:06.570 --> 00:19:07.680
in a primary region.

00:19:07.680 --> 00:19:09.930
In this case, US Central 1.

00:19:09.930 --> 00:19:11.970
Here again, we're using
synchronous replication

00:19:11.970 --> 00:19:14.370
within the region to ensure
that we can automatically

00:19:14.370 --> 00:19:17.340
failover without losing
committed transactions.

00:19:17.340 --> 00:19:19.530
Between regions or outside
of the primary region

00:19:19.530 --> 00:19:22.950
to your replicas, we're using
asynchronous replication.

00:19:22.950 --> 00:19:25.230
As you recall from our
terminology section,

00:19:25.230 --> 00:19:29.100
asynchronous replication means
the primary instance is not

00:19:29.100 --> 00:19:32.160
waiting for the replicas to
commit their transactions

00:19:32.160 --> 00:19:35.400
before it tells the client the
transaction has been committed.

00:19:35.400 --> 00:19:37.650
We do that because
the latency can

00:19:37.650 --> 00:19:40.830
be higher between regions,
given the geographic distance

00:19:40.830 --> 00:19:41.820
between them.

00:19:41.820 --> 00:19:43.740
We don't want to
harm performance

00:19:43.740 --> 00:19:46.650
in the primary region by
waiting for every replica

00:19:46.650 --> 00:19:50.260
around the world to say I also
have committed the transaction.

00:19:50.260 --> 00:19:52.630
And so we replicate
asynchronously.

00:19:52.630 --> 00:19:55.680
This has ramifications for
your recovery point objective.

00:19:55.680 --> 00:19:57.240
It means that you
can potentially

00:19:57.240 --> 00:20:00.060
have replication lag, usually
on the order of seconds,

00:20:00.060 --> 00:20:03.240
but your replica doesn't
have the exact real time

00:20:03.240 --> 00:20:05.190
copy of the data
set the primary has.

00:20:05.190 --> 00:20:06.930
It has a near real time copy--

00:20:06.930 --> 00:20:10.210
something that's perhaps a few
seconds in the past from what

00:20:10.210 --> 00:20:12.210
has now happened on
the primary instance.

00:20:12.210 --> 00:20:15.330
Bear this in mind, especially
in your disaster recovery plan.

00:20:15.330 --> 00:20:18.040
In this diagram though, we
see two replicas-- again,

00:20:18.040 --> 00:20:20.080
these could be in any
regions in the world,

00:20:20.080 --> 00:20:21.270
even in separate regions--

00:20:21.270 --> 00:20:24.210
allowing us to ensure that
we have a very near real time

00:20:24.210 --> 00:20:25.830
copy of data elsewhere.

00:20:25.830 --> 00:20:29.160
And that allows us, from an
RTO, recovery time objective

00:20:29.160 --> 00:20:31.560
perspective, in case
of a regional outage

00:20:31.560 --> 00:20:36.210
of our primary region, to
offer roughly a 1 to 3 minute

00:20:36.210 --> 00:20:38.490
recovery time,
where we can promote

00:20:38.490 --> 00:20:41.700
these replicas to
become read and writable

00:20:41.700 --> 00:20:44.880
to serve application traffic
in the event of an outage.

00:20:44.880 --> 00:20:48.030
It allows you to build a
database configuration that

00:20:48.030 --> 00:20:52.620
adheres to fairly strict RPO
and RTO objectives, or recovery

00:20:52.620 --> 00:20:56.010
time objectives, rather, in
case of a widespread or regional

00:20:56.010 --> 00:20:57.060
outage.

00:20:57.060 --> 00:20:58.920
One thing to note
here is the replicas

00:20:58.920 --> 00:21:01.000
themselves are always readable.

00:21:01.000 --> 00:21:03.390
So you are able, not
only to see which

00:21:03.390 --> 00:21:04.800
data has landed
on your replicas,

00:21:04.800 --> 00:21:08.190
but if you have use cases such
as reporting, or maybe you just

00:21:08.190 --> 00:21:10.800
want to offer lower
latency reads to users

00:21:10.800 --> 00:21:13.530
in other parts of the world,
cross region replicas are

00:21:13.530 --> 00:21:16.830
a good way to go
to accomplish that.

00:21:16.830 --> 00:21:19.920
To wrap up here, we
want to recommend

00:21:19.920 --> 00:21:23.860
that for especially critical
systems in your environment

00:21:23.860 --> 00:21:27.480
with Cloud SQL, you enable
automatic daily backups,

00:21:27.480 --> 00:21:29.160
and point-in-time
recovery for very

00:21:29.160 --> 00:21:32.130
granular or millisecond
level recovery.

00:21:32.130 --> 00:21:33.690
You should also
consider taking more

00:21:33.690 --> 00:21:35.250
frequent on-demand backups.

00:21:35.250 --> 00:21:36.892
These can be useful
if you're making

00:21:36.892 --> 00:21:38.100
for example, a schema change.

00:21:38.100 --> 00:21:39.600
You may want to take
an on demand backup

00:21:39.600 --> 00:21:41.310
just before you make
the schema change.

00:21:41.310 --> 00:21:42.685
Of course,
point-in-time recovery

00:21:42.685 --> 00:21:44.940
can be used to perform
that restoration as well,

00:21:44.940 --> 00:21:46.650
but it never hurts to
take an additional backup,

00:21:46.650 --> 00:21:48.192
especially when you
know you're going

00:21:48.192 --> 00:21:49.530
to be making a big change.

00:21:49.530 --> 00:21:53.370
Next, we encourage you to enable
in region high availability

00:21:53.370 --> 00:21:55.260
or synchronous
replication models.

00:21:55.260 --> 00:21:58.080
The Cloud SQL can health
check your instances,

00:21:58.080 --> 00:22:00.408
can automatically failover
in the event of an incident,

00:22:00.408 --> 00:22:01.950
and make sure that
you're back online

00:22:01.950 --> 00:22:03.600
without any intervention.

00:22:03.600 --> 00:22:05.910
And lastly, consider
using in region,

00:22:05.910 --> 00:22:09.060
or more importantly, cross
region replication, which

00:22:09.060 --> 00:22:10.860
is asynchronous
replication, to make

00:22:10.860 --> 00:22:12.690
sure you have a
near real time copy

00:22:12.690 --> 00:22:15.180
of your data in another
region then where

00:22:15.180 --> 00:22:16.980
your primary instance resides.

00:22:16.980 --> 00:22:19.740
That way in case of a
regional failure or disaster,

00:22:19.740 --> 00:22:21.540
you have a near real
time copy of data

00:22:21.540 --> 00:22:23.880
that can be made accessible
quite rapidly adhering

00:22:23.880 --> 00:22:27.310
to a fairly strict RTO.

00:22:27.310 --> 00:22:29.020
With that, I
encourage you to tune

00:22:29.020 --> 00:22:31.630
into two additional
sessions, first

00:22:31.630 --> 00:22:34.450
being data modernization,
which is McKesson's story

00:22:34.450 --> 00:22:36.460
of their migration to cloud.

00:22:36.460 --> 00:22:38.170
And then take a look
at our bare metal

00:22:38.170 --> 00:22:40.240
solution, which helps
you bring your enterprise

00:22:40.240 --> 00:22:42.490
workloads to GCP.

00:22:42.490 --> 00:22:45.880
If you want to see the showcase
that I mentioned inaction,

00:22:45.880 --> 00:22:48.980
please follow this link, and
play our wack-a-mole style,

00:22:48.980 --> 00:22:51.610
high availability demo.

00:22:51.610 --> 00:22:53.540
Lastly, try it out for yourself.

00:22:53.540 --> 00:22:57.340
Get hands on with Cloud SQL by
following our quick lab quest.

00:22:57.340 --> 00:23:01.120
You'll be introduced to a number
of Cloud SQL's capabilities,

00:23:01.120 --> 00:23:03.280
including those that
I mentioned today.

00:23:03.280 --> 00:23:06.200
And by the end, you'll be
quite familiar with Cloud SQL.

00:23:06.200 --> 00:23:08.530
With that, I want to thank
you for joining me today

00:23:08.530 --> 00:23:09.760
for this session.

00:23:09.760 --> 00:23:12.010
As mentioned, I'm available
for expert sessions

